w1 = 3 ≤ 4 ✓\nDependency: 0\n\n**Task 2: cmd/who differential tests**\nDependency: 1\n\n**Task 3: cmd/chroot implementation -- srd100-chroot R1.1, R1.2, R1.3**\nWeight check: 3 × w1 = 3 ≤ 4 ✓\nDependency: -1\n\nNow I need to think through what the test task actually requires. The differential tests should verify the who implementation by creating a test harness following the existing pattern and adding test cases that cover the output behaviors from the earlier requirements. Then I'll write out the full YAML configuration. - R3: Add test cases covering srd097-who R3.1-R3.3 output behaviors\n- R4: Ensure tests skip gracefully when gwho reference binary is not found\n\nThat's 4 requirements, which fits.\n\nFor each task, I need to identify design decisions, but only those actually derived from the SRD or existing architecture. The SIGPIPE handler comes from shared protocols, and the utmpx reading pattern is already established in cmd/users/ and cmd/pinky/, so those are valid. I should avoid inventing arbitrary choices and stick to what's documented.\n\nNow I'm verifying the file paths before writing the output: the SRD lives at docs/specs/software-requirements/srd097-who.yaml with the implementation at cmd/who/main.go and tests at cmd/who/who_test.go. For chroot, it's srd100-chroot.yaml with cmd/chroot/main.go. I need to make sure the required_reading references actual files that exist, since users and pinky are established commands I can safely reference.\n\nWriting the YAML output...\n\nActually, I'm second-guessing the code_status field. The \"not started\" label might just mean the code hasn't been regenerated in this run, not that it doesn't exist at all. Previous generation runs would have left the code in place, so pkg/testutils and other utilities are probably already there even if they're marked as \"not started\" in the current status tracking.\n\nSince pkg/ packages aren't showing up in requirement_states, that suggests they're already complete from earlier